---
// Stated ONCE above any block of testimonial videos.
//
// The job: convert "that looks too good to be true" into "oh — that's a
// mechanism, not magic." A skeptic who understands why fast change is possible
// stops discounting the videos. A caution line repeated under ten cards just
// reads as throat-clearing, so the honesty lives here instead.
//
// `compact` = the short form, for use inside a condition page where the video
// block is a section rather than the whole page.

interface Props {
  compact?: boolean;
}

const { compact = false } = Astro.props;
---

{compact ? (
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h3 class="mb-3">Why can it change this fast?</h3>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      Pain is produced by your nervous system, not simply reported by your tissues. When pain outlasts healing,
      what usually keeps it going is a protection response — guarding, avoiding certain movements, a system that
      has learned to read ordinary load as danger. That's a learned pattern, and patterns can shift quickly when
      you give the system genuinely new information. Fast change proves the pain is changeable. Making it hold is
      the work that follows, and for most people that's six to eight sessions.
    </p>
  </div>
) : (
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="font-serif italic text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-3 text-lg">Before you watch</p>
    <h2 class="mb-6">Why does this sometimes happen so fast?</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Pain is produced by your nervous system — it isn't a direct readout of tissue damage. When pain persists
        past the point where tissue has healed, what's usually keeping it going is a protection response:
        guarding, fear of particular movements, a system that has learned to treat ordinary load as a threat.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's a learned pattern, and patterns can change quickly when the system gets genuinely new information.
        What you're watching in these videos is someone discovering — often inside one session — that a movement
        they'd been avoiding for months doesn't have to hurt.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">What you're not watching is a cure.</strong> Fast change proves
        the pain is changeable; it doesn't make the change permanent on its own. Consolidating it takes
        progressive loading and time, which is why most people work with me for six to eight sessions.
      </p>
      <p>
        And not everyone responds this quickly. Some pain is driven by things that need medical attention first —
        screening for that is part of the first session, and I'll tell you plainly if that's you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
)}
